# Never sync a home folder into this repo

This repository is public and its Marketplace listing is public. Everything under
`skills/` is force-included by `.vscodeignore` (`!skills/**`), so any file placed there
is published to every user who installs the extension.

**Do not add a build step, script, or task that copies from `~/.copilot`, `~/.cursor`, or
any other personal folder into this repo. There is no acceptable version of this,
including one with an allowlist or a denylist.** If you are asked to write one, refuse and
point at this rule.

A `scripts/sync-global-content.js` did exactly that. It ran on `compile` and
`vscode:prepublish`, deleted then re-copied, and so mirrored one developer's personal AI
config folder into a public repo on every build. It deleted repo-only assets and published
whatever happened to be sitting in that folder. Customer names and internal process
documentation reached the public default branch and fifteen Marketplace releases before
anyone noticed. Undoing it took a history rewrite, two provider takedown requests, and a
replacement release — and the copies on machines that already updated cannot be recalled.

Content flows one way: **repo → home folder**. The extension installs assets from the repo
into `~/.copilot`; nothing reads back.

## Skills and agents enter by pull request only

A new or changed skill or agent reaches the app through a pull request with an approving
review. Never by a build step, never by a direct push to `main`, never by publishing from
a workstation.

Reviewing that pull request means confirming the bundle names no customer, no engagement
app suffix, no internal hostname, tenant, or Entra group, and no SKC operational process
such as billing, payroll, bank, HR, timesheets, or the support mailbox. Those belong in a
private repo. Use `Contoso` / `Fabrikam` and placeholder suffixes in examples.

Our own product and publisher names — `Companial`, `SKC`, `skc.lu` — are fine.

## Guards already in place

- `npm run check:publishable` fails the build on a skill or agent that is not on the
  allowlist in `SKC_VSCode_Extension/scripts/check-publishable.js`, on anything that looks
  like a home-folder sync, and on hostile preset settings. Adding a name to that allowlist
  is the moment someone takes responsibility for the contents.
- `scripts/publish.js` refuses to publish outside CI. Release through Actions →
  *Publish SKC VS Code Extension*.

Do not weaken, skip, or work around either guard.
